---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import { release } from '../../i18n/ui';
import Compare from '../../components/Compare.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import { existsSync } from 'node:fs';
import { join } from 'node:path';
const lang = 'en';
const hasInspect = existsSync(join(process.cwd(), 'public', 'blocks', 'denoise', 'inspect-en.png'));
---
<Doc lang={lang} page="blocks" current="Denoise" title="Denoise"
  description="The Denoise block of FLOAT: a Noise2Noise network that predicts one half-stack from the other, used as pretrained or adapted to the image, with its settings and limits.">

  <p class="kicker">Block</p>
  <h1>Denoise</h1>
  <p class="meta">FLOAT 0.2.0 · beta of {release.date.en}</p>

  <dl class="inshort"><div><dt>What it does</dt><dd>It removes most of the grain from the stacked image, before the colours are calibrated.</dd></div><div><dt>What it shows</dt><dd>A smoother sky and smoother faint nebulae; Inspect shows the image before and after, and the log says by how much the noise went down.</dd></div><div><dt>What can be set</dt><dd>Method: the default, Pretrained network, suits most nights; for a night without dithering the run advises Pretrained + adapted to the image, which takes minutes instead of seconds.</dd></div></dl>

  <aside class="note toc">
    <b>On this page</b>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#s1"><span class="secnum">1</span>Before and after on a real image</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s2"><span class="secnum">2</span>What goes in, what comes out</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s3"><span class="secnum">3</span>Noise2Noise between the half-stacks</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s4"><span class="secnum">4</span>The network</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s5"><span class="secnum">5</span>Pretrained, or adapted to the image</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s6"><span class="secnum">6</span>Four passes, and the sky level</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s7"><span class="secnum">7</span>Settings</a></li>
      <li><a href="#s8"><span class="secnum">8</span>Limits, and what was tried</a></li>
      <li><a href="#refs"><span class="secnum">&nbsp;</span>References</a></li>
    </ol>
  </aside>

  <div class="abstract">
    <p data-label="Summary">The Denoise block removes the noise of the stack with a Noise2Noise network: trained to
    predict one half-stack from the other, whose noise is independent, it learns to estimate the signal both share
    rather than a clean image it never saw. The network ships with FLOAT and runs in seconds; it can also first learn the noise of the image
    on its own half-stacks. FLOAT proposes the length of that adaptation from the noise it measures.</p>
  </div>

  <ol class="chain" aria-label="Position in the graph">
    <li><span>Astrometry</span></li><li><span>Gradient</span></li><li><span>Deconvolution</span></li>
    <li class="here"><span>Denoise</span></li><li><span>Color</span></li><li><span>Stars/background</span></li>
  </ol>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s1"><span class="secnum">1</span>Before and after on a real image</h2>
    <p>The comparison below uses one stack of the Rosette Nebula: 436 exposures of 15 s with a Sony A7S at ISO 4000, on a
    130/900 Newtonian. The two images come from the same project, without the Denoise block and with it in its default
    method, the pretrained network. Everything else is identical, and both are shown with the same stretch: the
    workflow layer of Post at Sky 0.5 and Faint tones 3.0. The sky’s level is unchanged to about one level out of 255
    (at most 1.2, measured as the median of the same faint tiles as Table 1); it only looks lighter on the denoised side
    because the dark grains are gone. The crops are at 100 %: one image pixel is one screen pixel. Drag the divider, or
    focus it and use the arrow keys. The block is added in <a href="/learn/">the tutorials</a>.</p>
    <div class="tbl-wrap wide">
      <table class="tbl">
        <caption><b>Table 1.</b>Noise in four zones of the stack, before and after Denoise. The figure is the spread of
        the pixel values in the faintest 30 % of the 16-pixel tiles of each zone, measured on the displayed 8-bit image
        (the stretch above, the same on both sides), in levels out of 255. The change is the reduction of that
        spread.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Zone</th><th>Box in the stack (x, y)</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">1 Bright nebula and dark globules</th><td class="n">1550–2250, 520–1220</td><td class="n">6.19</td><td class="n">2.67 (−57 %)</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">2 Faint nebula</th><td class="n">1936–2636, 241–941</td><td class="n">4.72</td><td class="n">1.39 (−71 %)</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">3 Star field</th><td class="n">2800–3500, 420–1120</td><td class="n">4.46</td><td class="n">0.97 (−78 %)</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">4 Sky (right of the ring, round stars)</th><td class="n">2750–3450, 1070–1770</td><td class="n">3.92</td><td class="n">0.83 (−79 %)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Compare lang={lang} n={1} before="/blocks/denoise/bright-nebula-before.png" after="/blocks/denoise/bright-nebula-after.png" width={700} height={700}
      alt="Zone 1, the bright nebula and dark globules: bright gas with dark globules, before and after Denoise.">
      Zone 1, bright nebula and dark globules. The bright gas is smooth and the dark globules read more clearly against
      it. The finest texture of the bright gas is a little softer. This is where Denoise removes the least noise (−57 %),
      because there is more signal there.
    </Compare>
    <Compare lang={lang} n={2} before="/blocks/denoise/faint-nebula-before.png" after="/blocks/denoise/faint-nebula-after.png" width={700} height={700}
      alt="Zone 2, the faint nebula: a faint arc of gas on the sky, before and after Denoise.">
      Zone 2, faint nebula. The faint arc stands out from the sky as a smooth veil, where before it was a grainy texture.
      Its shape and its edges are those of the stack.
    </Compare>
    <Compare lang={lang} n={3} before="/blocks/denoise/star-field-before.png" after="/blocks/denoise/star-field-after.png" width={700} height={700}
      alt="Zone 3, the star field: stars on the sky between them, before and after Denoise.">
      Zone 3, star field. The sky between the stars goes from mottled to even. The stars keep their size, their shape
      and their colour at this scale.
    </Compare>
    <Compare lang={lang} n={4} before="/blocks/denoise/sky-before.png" after="/blocks/denoise/sky-after.png" width={700} height={700}
      alt="Zone 4, the sky: stars on a dark background, before and after Denoise.">
      Zone 4, sky, to the right of the ring, where the stars are round. The coloured grain of the empty sky is gone, and
      the faint glow at the ring’s edge (left of the crop) can be seen. Every star, down to the faintest, is still there
      with its colour. The spikes of the brightest star are a little softer.
    </Compare>
    <p>The one limit seen in these four zones is in the bright gas of zone 1: its finest texture is a little softer,
    where the least noise is removed.</p>
    {hasInspect && (
      <Figure lang={lang} n={5} src="/blocks/denoise/inspect-en.png" alt="The Inspect page of FLOAT on the Denoise block, with its splitter between the image before and after." plate wide>
        The same comparison in the application: the Inspect page on the Denoise block, with its splitter.
      </Figure>
    )}
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s2"><span class="secnum">2</span>What goes in, what comes out</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>The noise map.</b> The drizzle weights say how many frames reach each pixel, so how much
    noise it holds. The block turns them into a map of the relative noise and gives it to the network beside the
    image.</aside>
    <p>The block reads the two half-stacks A and B (section 5 of the <a href="/blocks/stacking/#s5">Stacking</a> page),
    as the image blocks before it in the graph left them: with the sky gradient removed when the graph has a
    <a href="/blocks/gradient/">Gradient</a> block. It also reads the weight map of the stack.</p>
    <p>It writes the denoised stack and the network, with the background and the noise of each colour it measured. When
    a second Denoise block follows in the graph, it also denoises and keeps the two half-stacks for it. FLOAT reports
    the noise of the green channel before and after, and their ratio, the correlation of the noise between neighbouring
    pixels (section 5), and the number of adaptation steps. That noise is 1.4826 times the median absolute deviation of
    the linear green channel minus its Gaussian blur (σ 2 px), over every fifth pixel of the field. The preview shows a central square of the
    image before and after, side by side.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s3"><span class="secnum">3</span>Noise2Noise between the half-stacks</h2>
    <p>The two half-stacks show the same sky; their frame noise is independent. A network trained to predict one from
    the other cannot predict the noise of its target, which it does not see in its input; what it can learn is the
    signal common to both.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">[1]</a> It learns this without any clean image.</p>
    <p>During the adaptation (section 5) the pairs are used in both directions: for each tile, A predicts B or B predicts A, chosen at
    random.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s4"><span class="secnum">4</span>The network</h2>
    <p>A U-Net<a class="cite" href="#ref-2">[2]</a> of four levels, with 48, 96, 192 and 320 channels, and two 3 × 3
    convolutions with a skip connection in each block. Its input has four channels: the image of the three colours, from
    which the background of each colour is subtracted and which is divided by its noise and compressed by an arcsinh,
    and the noise map. Its output is a correction added to the normalised image. The background and the noise of each
    colour are measured on the half-stack (the median and 1.4826 times the median absolute deviation).</p>
    <p>The image is processed in tiles of 512 pixels with a margin of 48 pixels; a graphics card with little free memory
    gets smaller tiles, which can change the result at the seams of the tiles by a fraction of the noise. The full stack
    carries less noise than a half-stack: its noise map is divided by √2.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s5"><span class="secnum">5</span>Pretrained, or adapted to the image</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>Noise between neighbours.</b> A drizzled stack spreads each frame over neighbouring pixels,
    so the noise of one pixel resembles that of its neighbour. Without any shift between frames, nothing is spread, and
    the noise stays independent from one pixel to the next.</aside>
    <p><strong>Pretrained.</strong> The network that ships with FLOAT, applied as it is. It takes seconds on a graphics
    card and also runs on a processor. A monochrome stack of fewer than 30 frames gets a network made for monochrome
    noise.</p>
    <p><strong>Pretrained + adapted to the image.</strong> The same network first learns the noise of this image. It
    trains all its weights on random tiles of 192 pixels of the two half-stacks, in batches of 12; each tile is turned by
    a random multiple of 90 degrees, and the loss is weighted by the noise expected there. The learning rate follows a
    single cycle, then falls. The adaptation has two lengths:</p>
    <div class="tbl-wrap wide">
      <table class="tbl">
        <caption><b>Table 2.</b>The two lengths of the adaptation.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Length</th><th>Steps</th><th>Peak learning rate</th><th>Time on a graphics card</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Short</th><td class="n">500</td><td class="n">2 × 10<sup>−4</sup></td><td>about 40 s</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Long</th><td class="n">4000</td><td class="n">3 × 10<sup>−4</sup></td><td>about 5 min</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>On a processor, a step takes about 4 s.</p>
    <p><strong>Which length.</strong> FLOAT measures the correlation of the noise between horizontally neighbouring
    pixels, on the difference of the two half-stacks (green channel, a central square of 800 pixels, the sky only). It
    runs from about 0.15 to 0.4 for a dithered session, and near 0 without dithering. Under 0.08, FLOAT proposes the
    long adaptation, with the reason; otherwise the short one. A session without dithering has noise the pretrained
    network has not seen, and it needs the long adaptation.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s6"><span class="secnum">6</span>Four passes, and the sky level</h2>
    <p>The network leaves a faint pattern on its own grid of 2, 4 and 8 pixels. To spread it, the block runs the network
    four times, with the image moved by (0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 0) and (1, 1) pixels, and takes the mean. These four passes
    cancel the period of 2 pixels exactly and take the pattern as a whole to less than half. “Fast pass” runs one pass instead of four, four
    times faster; the pattern can then show in the sky under a strong stretch.</p>
    <p>The network moves the level of the sky by a small amount that differs from one colour to the next, and a strong
    stretch of a clean image shows it as a colour cast on the sky. The block measures, for each colour, the robust median
    of the difference between the result and the input over the covered pixels, and takes it off: the sky level of each
    colour stays what it was, and the objects of the field, which the network keeps, do not count in the measure.</p>
    <p>On a monochrome stack, the network moves the three channels slightly apart; the block replaces them by their
    mean, which keeps the image grey.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s7"><span class="secnum">7</span>Settings</h2>
    <div class="tbl-wrap wide">
      <table class="tbl">
        <caption><b>Table 3.</b>The settings of the block.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th><th>Range</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Method</th><td>Pretrained network</td><td>Pretrained network, Pretrained + adapted to the image</td><td>The network as it is, or first adapted to this image on its half-stacks (section 5).</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Adaptation</th><td>automatic</td><td>Short, Long</td><td>How long the network learns the image: 500 or 4000 steps. Long for a session without dithering. Proposed from the noise correlation, with the reason.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Fast pass</th><td>off</td><td>on, off</td><td>One pass of the network instead of four: four times faster, but its grid pattern can show in the sky under a strong stretch.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Random seed</th><td class="n">0</td><td class="n">0 or more</td><td>Seed of the random choice of the tiles of the adaptation, for runs that can be reproduced.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s8"><span class="secnum">8</span>Limits, and what was tried</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No dithering: the pretrained network is not enough.</strong> With the Pretrained method and a
      correlation under 0.08, the run warns that the noise is nearly independent between neighbouring pixels, that the
      network has not seen such noise, and that the Adapted method (long) denoises it much better.</li>
      <li><strong>A longer adaptation is not always better.</strong> On one of the test images, the long adaptation
      did slightly worse than the short one, and on another the pretrained network alone already did as well as
      either.</li>
      <li><strong>Few monochrome frames.</strong> The network for colour stacks turned the noise of monochrome stacks of
      few frames into dots that look like stars. A stack of fewer than 30 frames therefore gets its own network, which
      removes them and denoises as much, but brightens the faint stars by 8 to 11 % on the bench: it is for pictures, not
      for photometry. Only the Pretrained method uses it.</li>
      <li><strong>Training on each image was dropped.</strong> A network trained from zero on each image took about 21
      minutes on a graphics card and was unusable on a processor. The pretrained network, with or without the short
      adaptation, replaced it. The settings of that former method, saved in a project, are ignored.</li>
      <li><strong>The grid pattern.</strong> It is reduced, not removed: four passes take it to less than half, one pass
      leaves it whole.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="refs">References</h2>
    <ol class="refs">
      <li id="ref-1">Lehtinen, J. et al. 2018, <i>Noise2Noise: Learning Image Restoration without Clean Data</i>, ICML. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.04189">arXiv:1803.04189</a></li>
      <li id="ref-2">Ronneberger, O., Fischer, P. &amp; Brox, T. 2015, <i>U-Net: Convolutional Networks for Biomedical Image Segmentation</i>, MICCAI 2015, LNCS 9351, 234. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-24574-4_28">doi:10.1007/978-3-319-24574-4_28</a></li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</Doc>
